Z={
1:"Commentators note that Arjuna calls Kṛṣṇa by name here, a sign of earnest concern. Madhusūdana says the question is about those who neglect the rules through ignorance or laziness, but still worship sincerely. The answer will not be a simple verdict but a tool for self-examination.",
2:"Commentators note that faith is named first among the things coloured by the guṇas, before food, sacrifice, austerity and charity. Jñāneśvar compares faith to water, which takes the colour of the ground it flows over. The same longing for the sacred can become pure, restless or dark depending on the heart it passes through.",
3:"Commentators note sattvānurūpā, “in keeping with one’s inner being”: faith is not borrowed but grows from within. Madhusūdana says one can know a person by their faith. William James, the American psychologist, wrote that belief helps create the fact; the Gītā’s claim is older and deeper, that faith shapes the person.",
4:"Commentators note that the verse does not condemn worshippers but describes them. Śaṅkara says knowing the kind of worship tells one the guṇa at work. Village shrines to local spirits, the worship of wealth and power, and the worship of the gods are all found in Indian religious life. The Gītā offers a way to see which is which.",
5:"Commentators note ghora tapas, “terrible austerity”, not sanctioned by scripture. Madhusūdana lists examples: fasting unto death without cause, piercing the body, standing in fire. Indian law today forbids many dangerous practices. The Gītā’s rejection of them was a moral stand long before modern law.",
6:"Commentators note bhūta-grāmam, “the group of elements”, that make up the body. Śaṅkara says tormenting them torments the Lord within, who is their witness. Many modern teachers apply this to self-harm of any kind: neglecting health, overwork, harsh self-criticism. Respect for the body is respect for its indweller.",
7:"Commentators note that āhāras tv api sarvasya, “even food, of everyone”, is the first example: what we eat is the most frequent choice we make. Gandhi experimented with diet throughout his life, convinced that food shaped the mind. The Gītā’s three classes of food influenced his thinking.",
8:"Commentators note āyuḥ, life or longevity, named first among the effects of sāttvika food. Modern nutrition science stresses fresh vegetables, fruits, whole grains and moderation for long life, which agrees with much of this verse. Temple kitchens in South India still cook traditional sāttvika food for offering and distribution.",
9:"Commentators note that rājasika food causes duḥkha, śoka and āmaya: pain, grief and disease. Śaṅkara says the effects follow naturally from the food’s nature. Many Indian households avoid onion and garlic during fasts and festivals for this reason, considering them stimulating.",
10:"Commentators note that tāmasika food is also called amedhya, impure, unfit for offering. Food offered in worship must be fresh and clean, so tāmasika food is never offered. The practice of offering food first and eating the remainder, prasāda, naturally encourages fresh, sāttvika cooking.",
11:"Commentators note mana samādhāya, “with the mind fixed”: the sāttvika worshipper is fully present. Madhusūdana says the conviction “this ought to be done” is itself a kind of devotion. Many temple priests describe their daily rituals in exactly this spirit, done every day whether anyone watches or not.",
12:"Commentators note dambhārtham, “for show”, as one of two rājasika motives. Śaṅkara says such worship is done to be seen as religious. Grand weddings and festivals today are sometimes criticised in the same terms, when display overtakes devotion.",
13:"Commentators note śraddhā-virahita, “without faith”, as the final mark of tāmasika sacrifice. Śaṅkara says without faith, even correct rites bring no fruit. The list shows the five parts of a complete offering: right form, food shared, sacred words, a gift to the priest, and faith.",
14:"Commentators note śauca, cleanliness, as bodily austerity: keeping the body and surroundings clean is a spiritual practice. The Manusmṛti and later texts give detailed rules of daily cleanliness, treating them as part of dharma. Many Indian traditions begin worship with bathing and cleaning the place.",
15:"Commentators note anudvega-kara, speech that causes no agitation, as the first test. Śaṅkara says speech that hurts, even if true, is not austerity. The Tirukkuṟaḷ (100) says that speaking harsh words when sweet ones are available is like picking unripe fruit when ripe fruit hangs on the tree.",
16:"Commentators note saumyatva, gentleness, named after the moon, saumya. The moon gives soft light without burning. Śaṅkara says a gentle mind wishes well to all. Many teachers say mental austerity is the hardest because it cannot be seen, only lived.",
17:"Commentators note parayā śraddhayā, “with supreme faith”, and aphalākāṅkṣibhiḥ, “by those not desiring fruit”. Śaṅkara says these two transform ordinary discipline into sāttvika tapas. The same morning walk, prayer or fast becomes a different practice depending on the spirit behind it.",
18:"Commentators note satkāra-māna-pūjā, “respect, honour and reverence”, as the goals of rājasika austerity. Madhusūdana says such austerity seeks to be worshipped. Indian history has many examples of saints who fled fame, and some who sought it; the Gītā leaves no doubt which is better.",
19:"Commentators note mūḍha-grāha, “foolish obstinacy”: tāmasika austerity is stubborn rather than wise. Śaṅkara says it is undertaken from a deluded idea. The epics show such austerity bearing bitter fruit: the demons who gained boons through tapas were eventually destroyed by the very powers they sought.",
20:"Commentators note anupakāriṇe, “to one who cannot return the favour”: the purest gift expects nothing back. Śaṅkara explains deśa, place, as a holy place, and kāla, time, as a sacred occasion such as an eclipse or festival. Many people still give on special days, such as Akṣaya Tṛtīyā or during Kumbh Melā.",
21:"Commentators note parikliṣṭam, “with reluctance” or “painfully”: grudging giving spoils the gift. Śaṅkara says such giving is rājasika because it is done with an eye on return. Many traditions value a cheerful giver; St Paul writes that God loves one who gives cheerfully.",
22:"Commentators note asatkṛtam avajñātam, “without respect, with contempt”: the manner of giving matters. Śaṅkara says gifts thrown at the receiver, or given with insults, are tāmasika. In Indian tradition, a guest is treated as a god, atithi devo bhava; giving with contempt violates this ideal.",
23:"Commentators note brāhmaṇāḥ, vedāḥ and yajñāḥ, brahmins, Vedas and sacrifices, as ordained by this threefold name. Śaṅkara says the formula sanctifies them. Rāmānuja says the three words point to the Lord who gives all these their meaning.",
24:"Commentators note brahma-vādinām, “followers of Brahman”: those who seek the Supreme begin every act with Oṃ. The Taittirīya Upaniṣad (1.8) praises Oṃ: everything is Oṃ, and with Oṃ the priests begin their chants. Many Indian schools begin the day with a group chanting of Oṃ.",
25:"Commentators note mokṣa-kāṅkṣibhiḥ, “by seekers of liberation”: the word tat is for those who want freedom, not rewards. Madhusūdana says saying tat is a reminder that the act belongs to the Supreme. The Upaniṣads often point to Brahman as tat precisely because no name can capture it.",
26:"Commentators note sad-bhāve sādhu-bhāve ca, “in the sense of reality and of goodness”. Śaṅkara explains sat as existence and as virtue. In Indian thought, truth, goodness and reality are deeply linked. The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (3.1.6) says satyam eva jayate, “truth alone triumphs”, the motto of the Republic of India.",
27:"Commentators note tad-arthīyam, “action for that purpose”: any action done for the Lord is called sat. Rāmānuja gives examples such as building temples, gardens for the Lord’s worship and feeding devotees. Śaṅkara says even ordinary actions become sat when directed to the Supreme.",
28:"Commentators note na ca tat pretya no iha, “neither hereafter nor here”: faithless acts bring no fruit in either world. Madhusūdana says this is why faith must accompany every practice. The chapter ends by bringing faith back to the centre. The Gītā’s last chapter will gather all its teachings together.",
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
